###############################################################################

#
# This is the snug options file that provides the default command line options
# for the snug jar -> exe builder/launcher.
#
#  - This file will be loaded automatically when `snug` runs from this
#    directory. A `snug.options` sitting next to the `snug` executable
#    takes precedence over this one, so a portable `snug.exe` + its
#    defaults travel together.
#  - CLI flags will __ALWAYS__ override values in this file.
#  - `#`-prefixed lines are comments and are ignored
#  - Each line is parsed as shell-like tokens, so you __MUST__ quote any value
#    with whitespace, e.g. `--name "My App"`.
#
# Per-platform overrides
# ----------------------
# A `snug.macos.options` / `snug.windows.options` / `snug.linux.options`
# sitting alongside this file overrides it, highest precedence after the
# command line. It is a *partial* override - only the values that differ
# need to be in it, the rest still come from here - which is what the
# `--output` below needs: a macOS build has to name a `.app` bundle, not a
# Windows PE. Nothing writes that file for you; it is a deliberate,
# hand-maintained thing.
#
# You may also pass through a command line option which points to this file
# location - for example:
#
#    snug --options /path/to/snug.options
#
# `--options` means *that file only*: the per-platform tier is not consulted
# alongside it, so a build's options never depend on which machine ran it.
#
# Note: The file does not need to be named snug.options - it can be called
# anything you would like - but it is recommended to follow the convention
# and name it snug.options

--name "Snug JavaFX Demo"
--company "SynapticLoop"
--description "Snug JavaFX demo launcher"
--copyright "© SynapticLoop"
--version 1.0.0
--min-java 25
--main-class synapticloop.snug.demo.javafx.Launcher
--splash assets/snug-splash.png
--splash-ms 2000
--icon assets/snug-icon.png
--output assets/snug-javafx-demo.exe
#
# `auto` = "use a compatible JVM if one is already installed, and only
# offer the Temurin download if discovery comes up empty".
#
# It was `force`, which skips discovery entirely. On a machine that already
# has JDK 25 installed that meant downloading a second 115 MB copy of
# JDK 25 before the demo could start - and, worse, it meant starting the
# download flow at all, which on macOS currently hangs forever: the worker
# thread spins on `shared.started` (jdk_install.rs) until a human clicks
# Install in the progress window, and when the bundle is exec'd from a
# terminal rather than launched by Launch Services that NSAlert never
# appears. See AGENTS.md, "The download flow deadlocks on macOS".
--download-jdk=auto
#
# `ALL-UNNAMED`, not `javafx.graphics`. snug puts the fat JAR on the
# *classpath*, so JavaFX loads as part of the unnamed module rather than as
# a named module - which is why the JVM itself warns "Unknown module:
# javafx.graphics specified to --enable-native-access" and then does
# nothing. Naming a module that does not exist cannot grant anything.
# `ALL-UNNAMED` is the correct grant for a classpath-launched JavaFX, and it
# matters: JavaFX 25 calls `System.load` for its natives, which is a
# restricted method, and a future JDK will block it outright.
--jvm-arg --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED
